    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured MA worked solutions. No prior submission or grade is provided; confirmation rests on worked-solution evidence. q-76114: f(0)=1 and f'(0)=2, so the inverse derivative is 1/2. q-72913: setting y=0 gives 2(x-5)^2=3, yielding both stated intercepts. q-20061: dividing by 18 gives a·(b×c)=5; exchanging a and c reverses the sign. q-128356: a·(b×c)=18-5-12=1, so the requested product is -1. q-30627: the fundamental theorem of calculus returns the integrand at x. Its source writes dt while using integration variable s; this notation error does not change the intended answer. q-75583: g'(π/4)=(π/4) times the cube root of tan(π/4)=π/4. q-4553: the chain rule gives f'(2)=2√(4^3-4)=4√15. q-117289: differentiating both limits gives f'(1)=1/8-1/4=-1/8. The source's restriction that a fixed splitting point lie between x and √x is unnecessary and cannot hold throughout a neighborhood of 1; splitting at any fixed a gives the valid calculation. q-108630: a1=2S36/36-a36=3/4. q-5602: 1160=10(2a1-76), so a1=96. For the remaining series, S_N=N[2a1+(N-1)d]/2: q-5486 gives 4N²+5N=636 and N=12; q-108898 gives N(N+3)/4=52 and N=13; q-108904 gives N(N+7)/4=470 and N=40; q-67075 gives 7N²=175 and N=5; q-5446 has d=(60-4)/7=8, giving 4N²=1600 and N=20; q-108928 has d=3, giving 3N(N-3)/2=27 and N=6. Each count is the positive integer solution. The ± intercept choice represents the two separately stated source points, and the g' choice states the same value as the source.",
